These memory notes were rejected by an automatic machine-noise filter (CI/git/terminal signatures). Decide for EACH note: is it a legitimate durable memory, or actual machine noise?

REJECT (this is the default when in doubt) if the note is any of:
- raw terminal output or a tool dump (lines starting with "## <timestamp> — Tool", file:/// URLs, ANSI fragments);
- a progress/status line ("97.32% passed...", "Let me push: feat: ...", "688 tests, 0 failures");
- bare commands or commit/push instructions with no lived story around them;
- meta-commentary about the task in English ("The user asks...", "Let me check...") instead of a memory;
- a code/test dump with no narrative context.

APPROVE only if the note is a coherent memory OF THE OPERATOR'S DAY told in first person (Joan/me), even when it MENTIONS a command, a test result or the word "errors" as part of the story.

For every APPROVE, the reason MUST quote the narrative element (who told/asked what). If you cannot quote it, REJECT.

Return an ARRAY: [{"i": <index>, "approved": true/false, "reason": "<8 palabras>"}]

Notes:
[{"i": 0, "text": "nota $3 {y}"}]

Output ONLY the JSON array.
